## 4. INTERACTION AND MOTION PHILOSOPHY

Motion must communicate relationships (where something came from, what changed) and make the app feel responsive. It must never exist to show off, and must never slow everyday actions.

### 4.1 Rules
1. **Springs for anything the user touches or that can be interrupted** (official guidance: springs preserve velocity when the target changes; tweens respond less smoothly to interruption). **Tweens only for fades and fixed-duration colour/alpha changes.**
2. **Critically-damped by default** (`DampingRatioNoBouncy`). A tiny overshoot (`DampingRatioLowBouncy`) is allowed ONLY on gesture release (sheet fling, drag release). No bouncy, wobbly or cartoon motion anywhere.
3. **Fast in, faster out:** exits are shorter than entrances.
4. **One thing moves at a time** per interaction where possible. No staggered "showpiece" entrances on everyday screens.
5. **No infinite animations**, with one exception: a loading indicator while something is genuinely loading, which must stop under reduced motion.
6. **No animation on first-frame critical paths** (app open, SOS countdown screen).
7. Animations are driven by UI state only; business logic is never touched or delayed by an animation.

### 4.2 Motion table
| Interaction | Specification |
|---|---|
| Press on tappable surface/row/button | Scale to 0.97 with `snappy` spring on press, back on release/cancel. No ripple. Applied app-wide through one `Indication` (Section 7.3). |
| Toggle / switch / segmented selection | `snappy` spring on the thumb/indicator; selection haptic (Section 4.4). |
| Screen push (forward) | Incoming: slide in from about 25 % of width + fade in. Outgoing: slide about 15 % left + fade out. `standard` spring (settles in roughly 0.3 s — my calculation for a critically damped spring with stiffness 400, confirm by feel). |
| Screen pop (back) | Mirror of push. Predictive back must keep working (Section 9). |
| Top-level tab switch | Cross-fade + 2 % scale only. NEVER slide between top-level tabs. |
| Expand / collapse (rows, cards) | `Modifier.animateContentSize(standard spring)`; inner content fades with `fadeIn/fadeOut`. |
| List item insert / remove / reorder | `Modifier.animateItem()` with stable keys. |
| Bottom sheet / dialog | Sheet: system drag, `release` spring on fling settle; scrim fades with tween. Dialog: fade + 4 % scale-in. |
| Number text changes (static label) | Plain text swap. Do NOT animate every digit of a ticking timer. |
| Progress ring / bar | Draw-phase animation; one linear animation driven by real elapsed time; state read inside the draw lambda, not in composition. |
| Appear / disappear of content | `AnimatedVisibility` with fade + small vertical slide. |
| Success / error feedback | Haptic + brief colour/state change. No shake, no confetti. |

### 4.3 Reduced motion
Compose animations follow the system animation-duration scale (the "Remove animations" accessibility option sets it to 0) according to the community write-up I found; **verify on the emulator with animations off.** In addition, implement `rememberReducedMotion()` (Section 7.4) and use it so that, when reduced: screen transitions become a plain cross-fade, press-scale and decorative motion are removed, and nothing loops.

### 4.4 Haptics (restrained)
Use haptics only on: selection change (segmented control, tab), toggle on/off, confirmation (completing a focus session or routine task, saving a contact), rejection (invalid input, blocked action). NEVER on scroll, on every tap, or on loading. Use one helper (Section 7.4). **SOS-trigger vibration is a platform-layer behaviour — do not touch it.**

### 4.5 Safety-critical surfaces
The AR SOS countdown screen, readiness status, and anything shown over the lock screen get the plainest, fastest treatment: no blur, no glass, no entrance delay, maximum contrast, 56 dp+ touch targets, Cancel visually dominant, status communicated by text + icon + colour (never colour alone).

