## 7. COMPOSE ANIMATION AND PERFORMANCE INSTRUCTIONS

### 7.1 Motion tokens — REFERENCE SKETCH (compile-fix as needed)
```kotlin
object ArOsMotion {
    const val FadeInMs = 180
    const val FadeOutMs = 120

    /** Position/size changes the user can see travelling: expand, move, resize, screen push/pop. */
    fun <T> standard(): SpringSpec<T> = spring(Spring.DampingRatioNoBouncy, Spring.StiffnessMediumLow)
    /** Presses, toggles, selection. */
    fun <T> snappy(): SpringSpec<T> = spring(Spring.DampingRatioNoBouncy, Spring.StiffnessMedium)
    /** ONLY gesture release (sheet fling settle, drag release): a hair of overshoot. */
    fun <T> release(): SpringSpec<T> = spring(Spring.DampingRatioLowBouncy, Spring.StiffnessMediumLow)

    fun fadeIn(): TweenSpec<Float> = tween(FadeInMs, easing = LinearOutSlowInEasing)
    fun fadeOut(): TweenSpec<Float> = tween(FadeOutMs, easing = FastOutLinearInEasing)
}
```

### 7.2 Screen transitions — apply ONLY if the project uses Navigation Compose `NavHost`; otherwise use `AnimatedContent` with the same specs. Top-level tabs use cross-fade + 2 % scale, never a slide.
```kotlin
// REFERENCE SKETCH
enterTransition     = { slideInHorizontally(ArOsMotion.standard<IntOffset>()) { it / 4 } + fadeIn(ArOsMotion.fadeIn()) }
exitTransition      = { slideOutHorizontally(ArOsMotion.standard<IntOffset>()) { -it / 6 } + fadeOut(ArOsMotion.fadeOut()) }
popEnterTransition  = { slideInHorizontally(ArOsMotion.standard<IntOffset>()) { -it / 6 } + fadeIn(ArOsMotion.fadeIn()) }
popExitTransition   = { slideOutHorizontally(ArOsMotion.standard<IntOffset>()) { it / 4 } + fadeOut(ArOsMotion.fadeOut()) }
// When rememberReducedMotion() is true: use fadeIn/fadeOut only (no slide).
```

### 7.3 Press feedback — adapted from the official Indication migration page
```kotlin
// REFERENCE SKETCH — requires androidx.compose.foundation 1.7.0+.
// If the project's foundation is older, do NOT upgrade just for this: use a Modifier that reads
// collectIsPressedAsState() and applies animateFloatAsState inside graphicsLayer { scaleX/scaleY }.
object ArOsPressIndication : IndicationNodeFactory {
    override fun create(interactionSource: InteractionSource): DelegatableNode = PressNode(interactionSource)
    override fun hashCode(): Int = -1
    override fun equals(other: Any?) = other === this

    private class PressNode(private val source: InteractionSource) : Modifier.Node(), DrawModifierNode {
        private val scale = Animatable(1f)
        override fun onAttach() {
            coroutineScope.launch {
                source.interactions.collectLatest { i ->
                    when (i) {
                        is PressInteraction.Press -> scale.animateTo(0.97f, ArOsMotion.snappy())
                        is PressInteraction.Release, is PressInteraction.Cancel -> scale.animateTo(1f, ArOsMotion.snappy())
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        override fun ContentDrawScope.draw() {
            scale(scale.value, pivot = center) { this@draw.drawContent() }
        }
    }
}
// Theme root: CompositionLocalProvider(LocalIndication provides ArOsPressIndication) { ... }
```

### 7.4 Reduced motion and haptics — REFERENCE SKETCHES
```kotlin
@Composable
fun rememberReducedMotion(): Boolean {
    val context = LocalContext.current
    return remember {
        runCatching {
            Settings.Global.getFloat(context.contentResolver, Settings.Global.ANIMATOR_DURATION_SCALE) == 0f
        }.getOrDefault(false)   // setting missing = normal motion
    }
}

enum class ArOsHaptic { Tick, Confirm, Reject, ToggleOn, ToggleOff }

@Composable
fun rememberArOsHaptics(): (ArOsHaptic) -> Unit {
    val view = LocalView.current
    return remember(view) {
        val perform: (ArOsHaptic) -> Unit = { type ->
            val sdk = Build.VERSION.SDK_INT
            val c = when (type) {
                ArOsHaptic.Tick      -> if (sdk >= Build.VERSION_CODES.UPSIDE_DOWN_CAKE) HapticFeedbackConstants.SEGMENT_TICK else HapticFeedbackConstants.CLOCK_TICK
                ArOsHaptic.Confirm   -> if (sdk >= Build.VERSION_CODES.R) HapticFeedbackConstants.CONFIRM else HapticFeedbackConstants.CONTEXT_CLICK
                ArOsHaptic.Reject    -> if (sdk >= Build.VERSION_CODES.R) HapticFeedbackConstants.REJECT else HapticFeedbackConstants.LONG_PRESS
                ArOsHaptic.ToggleOn  -> if (sdk >= Build.VERSION_CODES.UPSIDE_DOWN_CAKE) HapticFeedbackConstants.TOGGLE_ON else HapticFeedbackConstants.CONTEXT_CLICK
                ArOsHaptic.ToggleOff -> if (sdk >= Build.VERSION_CODES.UPSIDE_DOWN_CAKE) HapticFeedbackConstants.TOGGLE_OFF else HapticFeedbackConstants.CLOCK_TICK
            }
            view.performHapticFeedback(c)
        }
        perform
    }
}
```
If any constant fails to compile for the project's `compileSdk`, substitute the nearest older constant and note it in the report. Haptics respect the user's system vibration/touch-feedback settings; that is expected.

### 7.5 Glass — two tiers
- **Tier 0 (default everywhere, no dependency):** near-opaque layered surfaces with hairline border and tonal separation. Translucent fills WITHOUT blur over scrolling text look muddy — so Tier 0 must be effectively opaque.
- **Tier 1 (optional, behind one kill switch):** true backdrop blur on AT MOST two persistent surfaces: the bottom bar and the collapsed large-title bar (and optionally sheet scrims). Requires API 31+ (the target phone qualifies); below that, or when the flag is off, render Tier 0. Build with Haze per Section 6.4.
```kotlin
object ArOsDesignFlags {
    /** Flip to false if jank appears on-device (Section 11.3). Everything falls back to Tier 0. */
    const val GlassBlur = true
}
```
- NEVER apply blur or shaders to: the SOS countdown screen, anything over the lock screen, full-screen scrolling content, or text-heavy areas.
- Default is ON only because the surface area is small; the on-device A/B test in Section 11.3 decides whether it stays.

### 7.6 Performance rules (apply everywhere)
1. **Read fast-changing state as late as possible.** Pass `State`/lambdas down; use `Modifier.graphicsLayer { }`, `offset { }`, `drawBehind { }` / `Canvas` for animated values so only draw/layout re-runs. Example: `val p by animateFloatAsState(target, ArOsMotion.standard())` then read `p` inside the `Canvas`/`drawBehind` lambda, not in composition.
2. **High-frequency state (timers, progress) must recompose the smallest possible node** — the single `Text` or the draw lambda — never the whole screen.
3. Lazy lists: stable unique `key` (never index or `hashCode`), `contentType` for mixed rows, `contentPadding` instead of outer padding, `animateItem()` for placement. No `list.indexOf(item)` inside `items`.
4. `remember` for expensive derivations only (sorting, formatting); hoist formatters and date formatters out of composition; do not allocate `Brush`/`Path`/`Shape` objects per frame.
5. For "threshold crossed" conditions (e.g. show the collapsed title) use `remember { computedStateOf { ... } }` if the project's Compose Runtime is 1.13+, otherwise `remember { derivedStateOf { ... } }`. Per the official page, `derivedStateOf` also stays right for expensive calculations read several times in one pass. Do not pass state read inside `derivedStateOf` as a `remember` key. Never write state after reading it in the same composition.
6. UI state classes are immutable; callbacks are stable. Prefer `Modifier.Node`-based custom modifiers over `composed { }`.
7. Shadows: tonal separation first; `Modifier.shadow` only on floating overlays, one at a time.
8. No `rememberInfiniteTransition` except the loading exception in 4.1. No more than one large animation running at once.
9. No large bitmaps; icons are vectors.
10. Do not add `@Stable`/`@Immutable` annotations speculatively; only fix measured or reported instability.
11. Debug builds are slower than release. Never judge smoothness from a debug build (Section 11.3).

